>> title: Onion-Tomato Masala
>> category: Sauces & Gravies
>> tags: indian, onion, tomato, base, curry
>> counters: Curry House
>> aka: base gravy, curry base, masala base, onion tomato base, bhuna masala, curry house base
>> pairs-with: ginger-garlic-paste, bhuna
>> servings: 6 cups
>> time: 1 hr 15 min
>> step.1: fry 25 min to a deep brown
>> step.2: fry in 2 min, until it stops smelling raw
>> step.3: bloom off the heat 30 sec
>> step.4: simmer 30 min, until the fat runs clear at the edge

Fry @yellow onions{2%lb}(900 g; halved and sliced thin) in @ghee{1/2%cup}(105 g) and @neutral oil{1/4%cup}(60 mL) in a wide #heavy pot{} ~fry{25%min}, stirring every few minutes, until they have collapsed to a third of their volume and gone the colour of a chestnut. This is the whole job. Onions pulled at pale gold make a base that tastes of raw onion for the rest of its life.

Fry @ginger-garlic paste{3%Tbs}(50 g) and @green chiles{2}(slit) into @&(~1)browned onions{} ~fry{2%min}, until the raw smell lifts and the paste stops spitting.

Bloom @ground coriander{1%Tbs}, @ground cumin{2%tsp}, @ground turmeric{1%tsp} and @Kashmiri chile powder{1%Tbs} in @&(~1)onion masala{} off the heat ~stir{30%sec} — the pot is hot enough, and ground spice in a pot on the flame is burnt spice.

Simmer @&(~1)spiced masala{} with @crushed tomatoes{28%oz}(800 g) and @kosher salt{1%Tbs} ~simmer{30%min}, until it has gone dark and jammy and a spoon drawn through it leaves a channel with clear orange fat standing at the edges.

A cup and a half is one curry for four. It keeps a week in the fridge and freezes in a tray of cubes.
